  "entity0": {
    "rationale": "Iteration 0 has no race telemetry yet, so this is a baseline built from the simulator contract. A five-sensor line array at 12 mm footprint spaced 10 mm apart spans 40 mm, wider than the 25 mm tape, so the line stays visible through moderate bends and the signal does not drop out between sensors. Sensors sit 0.05 m ahead of the axle so the controller sees the line before the wheels reach it, which gives the Integrator more lead time to correct. Wheel track 0.12 m and radius 0.03 m give a turn authority that matches the 350 PWM-per-turn-unit scaling without excessive yaw inertia. Mass 0.7 kg, with a body length of 0.16 m, keeps yaw inertia low for fast bend entry. Body width 0.12 m gives an off-track limit of max(0.06, 0.0125+0.03), which is 0.06 m; the 0.06 m floor dominates here, so body width does not widen the tolerance further. Expected result: completion on the oval and s-bend, with the slalom as the hardest test of the 5-sensor spacing. I expect rms error to be low enough that the line-loss count stays near zero.",
    "geometry": {
      "sensor_positions": [
        {"x": -0.02, "y": 0.05},
        {"x": -0.01, "y": 0.05},
        {"x": 0.0, "y": 0.05},
        {"x": 0.01, "y": 0.05},
        {"x": 0.02, "y": 0.05}
      ],
      "sensor_size": 0.012,
      "wheel_track": 0.12,
      "wheel_radius": 0.03,
      "body_width": 0.12,
      "body_length": 0.16,
      "mass": 0.7
    },
    "intent": "Establish a symmetric, forward-mounted 5-sensor baseline with a 40 mm sensing span covering the 25 mm tape, a moderate 12 cm track for turn authority, and low mass for quick yaw response. The Integrator can build a weighted-average controller on it. Next iterations will adjust sensor spacing, forward offset, and track width based on whether sensing saturates (readings all near 0 or 1 on bends) or the robot oscillates too much.",
    "feedback_request": {
      "questions": [
        "Do the outer sensors ever read near 0 or 1 at the same time, indicating the 40 mm span is too wide or too narrow for the tape?",
        "Is the steering signal saturated on tight bends, and does a 0.12 m track give enough turn authority?",
        "Does the forward offset of 0.05 m cause lag or overshoot, or is it helpful?",
        "Does the line-loss count come from entry into the slalom or from the s-bend transition?"
      ]
    },
    "learning": "No race data yet. Hypothesis to test: a 40 mm sensing span with 12 mm sensors gives smooth signal interpolation on bends, while a 0.05 m forward offset improves stability at speed. If line loss happens at the slalom, the next step is to widen the span or add sensors.",
